From: GloriousEggroll <gloriouseggroll@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] winepulse: send DualSense haptics and speaker audio to Bluetooth controllers.

A Bluetooth DualSense has no USB audio function, so games find no
four-channel controller endpoint for HD haptics. When winebus presents
the controller as USB (Steam Input disabled), expose a synthetic
48 kHz four-channel endpoint for it with the same container ID, and send
it to the controller through hidraw, paced in real time:

- Channels 3 and 4 (haptics) are resampled to 3 kHz, 8-bit, 64 bytes
  per report.
- Channels 1 and 2 (speaker or headphones) are encoded as one 10 ms,
  200 byte CBR Opus frame per report, using libopus loaded at runtime.
  The headphone jack state from the input reports selects the speaker
  or headphone output.

While speaker audio plays, report 0x36 carries both, with a state block
that selects the speaker (or headphone) output path and volume: over
USB the controller routes its audio function by itself, over Bluetooth
nothing plays until the path is set. Otherwise report 0x32 carries
haptics only. Without libopus only haptics are sent.

Games may open several streams on the endpoint (Assassin's Creed Shadows
opens two); they are mixed on a shared clock into one report sequence
per controller, with one Opus encoder and one set of counters.
PROTON_DUALSENSE_BT_HAPTICS=0 disables it.

Packet layout and resampling follow DS5Dongle
(https://github.com/awalol/DS5Dongle) and vDS
(https://github.com/hurryman2212/vds), both MIT licensed.
